1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,736
Now the hard one. A dive club books for a group.

2
00:00:03,816 --> 00:00:09,976
Ten guests, four nights, five twin rooms, full board, airport
transfer — and they mention diving.

3
00:00:10,056 --> 00:00:12,712
All of that lands in one message.

4
00:00:12,792 --> 00:00:17,128
Six of the eight booking fields are captured from that single
message.

5
00:00:17,208 --> 00:00:23,320
The assistant confirms what it understood, and asks only about the
diving schedule it was not given.

6
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,960
Here is the part that used to break.

7
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:33,136
The whole group does not dive the same days. Six dive for four days;
the other four dive for two.

8
00:00:33,216 --> 00:00:40,864
There is no single number that describes that, so the assistant
keeps Marco's own words as the schedule rather than inventing one.

9
00:00:40,944 --> 00:00:44,800
And this is the boundary that matters most on a group booking.

10
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:54,664
A partner rate is a commercial decision, so the assistant does not
quote it and does not decline it. It routes the request to a person
and says so.

11
00:00:54,744 --> 00:01:00,616
The guest gets a fast, complete answer. Every decision about money
stays with your team.
